</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Toto<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 1:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Skiller  - I&#39;m with Featherw - that cracked me up. LOL &#40;-: <BR> <BR>Flytodeb - thanks for the clarification. &#40;I knew it was too good to be true&#41;.   <BR> <BR>I&#39;ll be watching, but I&#39;m not holding my breath for a really significant clue.... <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7057-->
<!--Post: 7059--><!--Time: 1108159389-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7059"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 2:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Flyt, I bet you are right.  If KAKE never touched the box, WPD got to it first and there may never be an exact report on what was in the box.  Would have been nice if a reporter could have gotten close enough to the box to peek inside it...but from what I recall from the pics of the box, it was stuffed with some crepe paper that stuck out of the box.  So...I guess without touching it, noone could peek inside to see what was in it. <BR> <BR>So KAKE is only going to reveal what was typed on the postcard?  I am with Toto...I am not holding my breath for a major clue from that postcard.  It will probably be another form of his NOVEL THINKING for us to figure out...or a new way to tell WPD they are &#39;way off bases&#39;. lol.. <BR> <BR>Thanks for the info..<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7059-->
<!--Post: 7078--><!--Time: 1108172766-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7078"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 5:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Another article mentioning the hacking at WSU: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/10861834.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/10861834.htm</a> <BR>WSU says hacker gained access to three servers <BR>Posted on Thu, Feb. 10, 2005 <BR>Officials think the person may have intended to use university servers to store music and movie files for downloading by others. <BR> <BR>No credit card numbers were in the files, but there were Social Security and passport numbers in some cases. <BR> <BR>The university sent letters or e-mails to about 7,500 people whose information was in the databases to alert them to watch for identity theft, said Peter Zoller, WSU&#39;s chief information officer. He said the university has not heard of any complaints of identity theft. <BR> <BR>----- <BR>ID theft? Whew....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7078-->
<!--Post: 7081--><!--Time: 1108173799-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7081"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 11, 2005 - 6:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I note the mention of a particular DEPT. that was hacked... <BR> <BR>Speech-Language-Hearing Clinic <BR> <BR>Here is a site link to that DEPT at WSU: <BR><a href="http://webs.wichita.edu/?u=cds&amp;p=index" target=_top>http://webs.wichita.edu/?u=cds&amp;p=index</a> <BR>  <BR>Welcome to the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders &#40;CSD&#41; <BR>================================================ <BR><a href="http://webs.wichita.edu/?u=vbain&amp;p=/index" target=_top>http://webs.wichita.edu/?u=vbain&amp;p=/index</a> <BR>Welcome to the Wichita State University <BR>Speech-Language-Hearing Clinic <BR>  <BR>SPEECH-LANGUAGE .  <BR>Accent Modification. <BR>Aphasia Group. <BR>Augmentative/Alternative. <BR>Aural Rehabilitation. <BR>Autism Spectrum Clinic. <BR>Diagnostic Clinic. <BR>Fluency Disorders Program. <BR>Kaleidoscope. <BR>Language Literacy Group. <BR>Phonology Clinic. <BR>Speech-Language Fees. <BR>Tongue Thrust. <BR>======================================== <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/8635745.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/8635745.htm</a> <BR> <BR>The department of modern and classical languages had 14 full-time professors when she started in 1991. It now has nine. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7081-->
<!--Post: 7191--><!--Time: 1108268694-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7191"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 8:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Look at Bill Thomas on this page... <BR> <BR><a href="http://webs.wichita.edu/ielc/facultybio-IE.html" target=_top>http://webs.wichita.edu/ielc/facultybio-IE.html</a> <BR> <BR>Mr. Bill Thomas obtained his Masters degree in linguistics from Indiana University in Bloomington, Indiana. Teaching at Wichita State in the 1970’s, Mr. Thomas left for California to work as a teacher for the Xerox Company. In 1991, he returned to teach English again, and now concentrates on Speaking and Listening classes. In addition, Mr. Thomas coordinates the New Student Orientation and Wednesday Conversation classes for the Intensive English Language Center. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7191-->
<!--Post: 7194--><!--Time: 1108270782-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7194"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skiller<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skiller<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 8:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Featherw  Keep up the good work Im kind of doing a profile search on a few people. something is drawing me to clearwater Ks  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7194-->
<!--Post: 7201--><!--Time: 1108273757-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7201"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 9:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks Skiller.  Clearwater...hmmmmm.. <BR> <BR>Note in the above link that the Bill Thomas mentioned has a connection to linguistics and taught at the XEROX co.  <BR> <BR> Note in the articles below the mentions of BTK&#39;s use of a WSU copy machine to copy a poem to Professor WYATT...and BTK was comfortable enough as a STUDENT at WSU to use the copier....Also note the request to look at the poem&#39;s...LINGUISTICS... <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/10309381.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/10309381.htm</a> <BR> <BR>Continued links to WSU <BR> <BR>Word that BTK claims to have repaired copiers and business equipment prompted officials at Wichita State University to review records to see whether they could determine who worked on copiers and business equipment at the university in the 1970s. <BR> <BR>&#34;There&#39;s no luck as of now, but we&#39;re going to continue our efforts&#34; today, said R Lowe, WSU&#39;s vice president for operations and finance. <BR>==================== <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/archive/9458680.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/archive/9458680.htm</a> <BR>Police seek tips on BTK-professor link <BR>Posted on Sat, Aug. 21, 2004 <BR>Beattie said a former detective who investigated the killings during the 1970s told him that investigators interviewed Wyatt to see whether she could recognize the poem&#39;s linguistics.  <BR> <BR>BTK used a WSU copying machine for a 1978 letter he sent. He reportedly made a reference to the school&#39;s campus to the survivor of one attack. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s use of the WSU copier indicates he felt comfortable using the campus, McCrary said. Told of the other links between BTK and WSU, McCrary said it&#39;s clear why the investigation seems to be focusing at least partly on WSU connections. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7201-->
<!--Post: 7216--><!--Time: 1108286609-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7216"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 1:23 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Gee...looks like I am pigging this thread. ha! <BR> <BR>Anyway...Clearwater, Kansas. <BR> <BR>I notice a mention of the  freight train wagon route to Indian Territory...an easy crossing of the Ninnescah &#40;an Osage-Sioux name meaning &#34;water clear&#34;&#41; <BR> <BR>OSAGE street...Was it Osage that BTK mentioned in one of his earlier posts? <BR> <BR><a href="http://skyways.lib.ks.us/towns/Clearwater/" target=_top>http://skyways.lib.ks.us/towns/Clearwater/</a> <BR>History <BR>In 1865, Jesse Chisholm broke the trail for a freight train wagon route to Indian Territory and made an easy crossing of the Ninnescah &#40;an Osage-Sioux name meaning &#34;water clear&#34;&#41; River southeast of the present town of Clearwater. The town was founded because of the trail and today has a population close to 2,000. The first cattle drive on the trail took place in 1867. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7216-->
<!--Post: 7279--><!--Time: 1108352717-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7279"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 13, 2005 - 7:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oh how interesting... a BTK and maintenance of FREIGHT cars...probably a coincidence..but interesting... <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.wfk-kl.de/englisch/news/news01.html" target=_top>http://www.wfk-kl.de/englisch/news/news01.html</a> <BR>Business Notes <BR>BTK <BR> <BR>The Bahntechnik Kaiserslautern GmbH build a new workshop. The investment of about 3,1 million € will be subsidized by funds of the European Union, the Federal Government and the State of Rheinland-Pfalz in the amount of 300.000 €.  <BR> <BR>The BTK is active in corrective repair, maintenance and modernization of freight cars and components. The central plant for rework, repair and maintenance of train equipments of the Keystone Europe group &#40;BTK belong to&#41; is located in Kaiserslautern. Most important customer is the Deutsche Bahn AG. Keystone Europe is the largest company group of the American corporate group Amstedt Industries in Europe. Head office is in Chicago. Amstedt Industries is represented in 11 countries with altogether 9.200 employees. BTK is one of the biggest production facilities of Kaiserslautern with 250 employees. The new workshop will secure 14 job positions.  <BR>========================================== <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7279-->
<!--Post: 8050--><!--Time: 1108841599-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8050"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 19, 2005 - 11:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://weberstudies.weber.edu/archive/archive%20A%" target=_top>http://weberstudies.weber.edu/archive/archive%20A%</a> 20%20Vol.%201-10.3/Vol.%207.1/7.1skeen.htm  <BR> <BR>Anita SKEEN poetry...another page...  <BR> <BR>Humanities Seminar  <BR> <BR>Excerpted from the page:  <BR>4.  <BR> <BR>A woman drives south down the interstate  <BR>to see her lover.  <BR>She suspects this is the last  <BR>time. She concentrates hard.  <BR>She turns all their names over  <BR>again and again  <BR>like unfamiliar rocks.  <BR>She procounces them sofly,  <BR>sifts the vowels from the consonants.  <BR>================== <BR> <BR>Author Name:   Skeen, Anita. <BR> <BR>Title:    Each Hand a Map.  <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.junebugbookstore.com/si/12012.html" target=_top>http://www.junebugbookstore.com/si/12012.html</a> <BR>  <BR>Keywords:    Poetry Lesbian Poetry Gay &amp; Lesbian Anita Skeen  <BR> <BR>==================================== <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8050-->
